{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789384440,"nonce":"TkD1TMRN2uZfJHtjlgZyLE/8iAc=","title":"Floor division and remainder by a constant cost five extra operations","message":"The SSA builder lowers `FloorDiv`/`FloorRem` on integers as truncating\n`div`/`rem` plus a correction: `rem != 0 \u0026\u0026 (rem \u003c 0) != (d \u003c 0)`,\nthen a select. With the divisor a constant, most of that is known:\n\n- a positive power of two: `x // 2^k` is `x \u003e\u003e k` (arithmetic) and\n  `x % 2^k` is `x \u0026 (2^k - 1)`, for either sign of `x`;\n- any constant of known sign: the correction is one compare of the\n  remainder against zero.\n\nPython's `//` and `%` are floor operations, so every `n % 2`,\n`x // 2` and `acc % 1000003` in a Python kernel took the long form.\n`bench_collatz.py` 90 to 67.5 ms (ZynML, truncating semantics, 75;\nPyPy 99). `bench_branchy.py` is unchanged at 98 ms: its two `rem`\nper iteration are the loop's dependency chain and the remaining\ncompare and select sit on it.\n\nConformance `binop/floor_by_powers_of_two.py`.","files":null}]}